# Source: Slides "ICT3112 - 5 - Conditional Rule", worked example.
id: conditional-slides
title: A conditional with precondition x + y = 0
topic: Conditional
level: 2
source: Slides "ICT3112 - 5 - Conditional Rule", worked example
tags: conditional, wp, consequence

task:
  Calculate the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition
  $Q$: first the weakest precondition of each branch, then the one of the
  conditional. Then use the consequence rule to decide whether the triple
  is valid.

given:
  Condition B: $x = y$

pre: x + y = 0
post: x = y + 1

program:
  {P}
  {R3}
  if (x = y) then {
    {R1}
    x := y + 1        // then
    {Q}
  } else {
    {R2}
    x := 1 - x        // else
    {Q}
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of the then branch x := y + 1
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: then
text:
  Each branch ends where the conditional ends, so its postcondition is
  $Q$. We substitute the assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment
  expression ($y + 1$) in $Q$.
calc:
  wp(x := y + 1, Q)
  = (x = y + 1)[x <- y + 1]
  = y + 1 = y + 1          // x is replaced by y + 1
  = true                   // both sides are the same
set: R1 = true

step: R2, the weakest precondition of the else branch x := 1 - x
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: else
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment
  expression ($1 - x$) in the postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(x := 1 - x, Q)
  = (x = y + 1)[x <- 1 - x]
  = 1 - x = y + 1          // x is replaced by 1 - x
  = -x = y                 // subtract 1 from both sides
  = x + y = 0              // add x to both sides
set: R2 = x + y = 0

step: R3, the weakest precondition of the conditional
rule: IF, WP
focus: if, R1, R2
text:
  The weakest precondition of `if B then c1 else c2` for $Q$ is
  >> (B ==> wp(c1, Q)) && (!B ==> wp(c2, Q))
  Here the condition $B$ is $x = y$, $wp(c1, Q)$ is $R1$ and
  $wp(c2, Q)$ is $R2$:
  >>> («x = y : B» ==> «R1») && («!(x = y) : ¬B» ==> «R2»)
calc:
  wp(if (x = y) then x := y + 1 else x := 1 - x, Q)
  = (x = y ==> true) && (!(x = y) ==> x + y = 0)   // B, R1 and R2 put in the formula
  = true && (!(x = y) ==> x + y = 0)               // A ==> true is true
  = !(x = y) ==> x + y = 0                         // true && A is A
  = x != y ==> x + y = 0                           // !(x = y) is x != y
set: R3 = x != y ==> x + y = 0

step: P implies R3
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R3
text:
  $R3$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R3$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R3»
vc: x + y = 0 ==> (x != y ==> x + y = 0)
why:
  Assume $P$, that is $x + y = 0$. This is the right side of the
  implication $R3$, so $R3$ holds.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The then branch establishes $Q$ from any state. The else branch needs
  $x + y = 0$, which is exactly the precondition $P$.
